1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:04,771
Two runs off. But fielding is
where I can't tell you the whole
story.

2
00:00:04,821 --> 00:00:08,834
I'm building CricPredict: a
cricket prediction app for fans.

3
00:00:08,884 --> 00:00:11,000
Entertainment only, not betting.

4
00:00:11,350 --> 00:00:14,875
First ODI of India's 2026 tour of
New Zealand.

5
00:00:14,925 --> 00:00:21,249
Virat Kohli took a one-handed
diving catch at second slip to
dismiss Nathan Smith.

6
00:00:21,299 --> 00:00:24,435
Rohit Sharma made a sharp run-out
assist.

7
00:00:24,485 --> 00:00:27,000
Moments like these swing a match.

8
00:00:27,350 --> 00:00:35,200
At 84/4 after 17 overs,
CricPredict predicted 199/7.

9
00:00:35,550 --> 00:00:37,042
New Zealand finished 185 all out:

10
00:00:37,092 --> 00:00:39,800
fourteen runs lower, but three
wickets more than predicted.

11
00:00:40,150 --> 00:00:45,400
Later, at 171/9 after 37.3 overs,
it predicted 183 all out.

12
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:53,300
Two runs off. Encouraging, but a
much shorter forecasting horizon.

13
00:00:53,650 --> 00:00:57,473
Did fielding explain the gap? I
can't prove it.

14
00:00:57,523 --> 00:01:00,274
But the model has room to improve:

15
00:01:00,324 --> 00:01:05,300
in my backtest on men's ODIs, its
average error is 41.5 runs.

16
00:01:05,650 --> 00:01:08,677
Fielding is one thing it can't see
properly.

17
00:01:08,727 --> 00:01:15,600
Today it is just one hand-assigned
rating per team, a reputation, not
who is actually on the field.

18
00:01:15,950 --> 00:01:28,200
I think that is part of what's
holding accuracy back, on runs and
on wickets.

19
00:01:28,550 --> 00:01:36,511
Public ball-by-ball data names a
fielder only when a wicket falls,
including run-outs from direct
hits.

20
00:01:36,561 --> 00:01:44,600
It doesn't show dropped catches,
runs saved by brilliant effort, or
how difficult a catching chance
was.

21
00:01:44,950 --> 00:01:46,258
So here is the plan.

22
00:01:46,308 --> 00:01:57,601
Start with what public data does
give me, the catches and run-outs
that end in a wicket, and build a
first fielding model from that in
shadow mode, knowing its limits.

23
00:01:57,651 --> 00:02:03,918
Then, if I can get ball-by-ball
fielding data, use it to rate the
actual fielders in each XI.

24
00:02:03,968 --> 00:02:08,332
Test the effect on runs and
wickets separately, on fresh
matches.

25
00:02:08,382 --> 00:02:12,000
If it doesn't beat the current
model, it doesn't ship.

26
00:02:12,350 --> 00:02:16,622
If you hold fielding data, or work
at a data provider, message me.

27
00:02:16,672 --> 00:02:19,700
Try CricPredict at
cricket-score-predictor.com.
